   <indicator ascending="1" measure="2">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Percent reduction in number of children with reported cases of diarrhea in the last two weeks, in beneficiary communities</narrative>
    </title>
    <baseline iso-date="2011-06-13" value="11.64" year="2011">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">AF: Indicator added as part of the restructuring under the AF packageBaseline for this indicator based on first measurement ofimpact evaluation</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2011-06-13"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2013-06-30"></period-end>
     <target value="10.50"></target>
     <actual value="16.80">
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">Indicator partially achieved. Source: impact evaluation (Nov 2010 - Sept 2012). Note that this indicator was added in 2011 (withthe design of the additional financing). In the impact evaluation baseline was measured as 13.4% (not 11.64 as noted to the left)in Nov 2010. One reason for the lack of impact could be that mothers&apos; knowledge of diarrhea risk factors and symptoms increasedduring this period, potentially leading to higher reporting. Furthermore,  the increase in diarrhea may be due to factors beyondthe controlof AIN-C (e.g. contaminated water). This is corroborated by the fact that there was an increase in zinc consumption,which can prevent diarrhea, during this time, without an accompanying reduction in diarrhea.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
